  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA deep upper level low moving into the Pacific Northwest will \ncontinue to funnel moisture into North Central and Southwestern \nMontana today on southwesterly flow aloft. Early afternoon radar \nhas shown numerous showers and thunderstorms developing across \nSouthwestern Montana, with a few also developing over the Rocky \nMountain Front and Little Belt and Snowy Mountains. While severe \nwind gusts are not expected today, I can\u2019t entirely rule out a \nstronger storm producing a rogue gust over 50 mph or so, along \nwith some heavier downpours, especially if they can get a bit more\norganized. The most likely area for the winds to get gusty will \nbe across the plains, where clouds will hold off just long enough \nto allow for some better thermal mixing and deepening of the mixed\nlayer. While storms should weaken after sunset, showers with a \nfew rumbles of thunder will remain possible through much of the \novernight thanks to persistently strong upper level dynamics. \n\nAs we head into the day on Sunday, winds shift to the west as the\nupper level low moves overhead, which will bring a surface cold \nfront along with it late tonight/early Sunday morning. Behind this\nfront, expect temperatures to be notably cooler tomorrow, with \nmost locations only rising into the 60s, along with a breezy \nwesterly wind with most areas on the plains gusting to 25-40 mph \nand 15-30 mph across the southwest. \n\nWhile it will remain breezy on the plains/Rocky Mountain Front \novernight Sunday night into Monday morning, expect more pleasant \nconditions to arrive for the daytime on Monday as upper level \nridging begins to build back across the Northern Rockies. While \nthis ridge will keep us dry on Monday, it will quickly move east \nas a strong trough moves towards the West Coast. As this trough \napproaches, southwesterly flow aloft will bring a surge of \nmoisture into the area, resulting in afternoon thunderstorm \ndevelopment Tuesday and Wednesday. A stronger upper level low will\nlikely move towards the Pacific Northwest late next week, which \nwould allow for additional showers and thunderstorms Thursday and \nFriday.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nRain today: Some of the convection allowing models have wanted to\nhave some pockets of heavier rainfall (up to an inch) from some \nof the showers and thunderstorms today. While our soils should be \nable to readily absorb these heavier rainfall amounts, repeated \nheavy rainfall may result in some ponding of water in poor \ndrainage areas. \n\nWinds Sunday: Behind the cold front mentioned above, expect gusty\nwinds to affect most of North Central and Southwestern Montana on\nSunday. While cooler temperatures, recent rainfall, and RH values\ngenerally staying above 25-30% will prevent critical fire weather\nconditions, wind gusts will still be notable for late August. \nExpect an 80-90% chance of seeing wind gusts over 40 mph along the\nRocky Mountain Front, northern slopes of the Little Belt and \nSnowy Mountains, and around the Bears Paw Mountains, and a 30-60% \nchance of seeing gusts over 48 mph. While not impossible, the risk\nof seeing high wind gusts (over 58 mph) appears to be low, \ngenerally under 10-20%, I can not completely rule out an isolated\ninstance of high winds tomorrow. Ludwig",
